-- allium: 3
-- doctor.allium
--
-- Scope: `dispatch doctor` self-diagnosis CLI surface
-- Covers: findings model, check surfaces, flag conventions, output contract
--
-- The repo-sync check's subject matter (the ahead/behind measurement, the sync
-- operation and its failures) is specified in repo-sync.allium; only its
-- doctor-side reporting lives here.

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Enumerations
------------------------------------------------------------

enum FindingStatus { ok | warn | error }

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Entities
------------------------------------------------------------

-- A Finding is one detected condition from a single check run.
-- status drives the exit code on a plain check run (any warn or error → exit 1),
-- but the repair paths override that; see the Output contract section.
entity Finding {
    check:            String     -- which check produced this finding: "worktrees" | "sessions" | "hooks" | "repo-sync"
    status:           FindingStatus
    target:           String     -- the specific path/window/repo being described
    message:          String     -- human-readable one-line description
    repair_available: Boolean
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- CLI Surface
------------------------------------------------------------

-- `dispatch doctor [SUBCOMMAND] [FLAGS]`
--
-- Subcommands (all default to detect-only):
--   worktrees   compare .worktrees/ dirs against tasks.worktree in the DB
--   sessions    compare tasks.tmux_window against live tmux windows
--   hooks       verify git config core.hooksPath = .githooks in each known repo
--   (none)      run every check in sequence: worktrees, sessions, hooks,
--               repo-sync
--
-- repo-sync has no subcommand form of its own; it runs only as part of the
-- bare all-checks form, and it is never repairable — closing drift is an
-- explicit operator action (see repo-sync.allium: SyncNeverAutomatic), so it
-- can never be something --repair applies on the operator's behalf.
--
-- "Known repo" (the repo set every check runs over) is the union of the saved
-- repo paths and the distinct repo_path of every task, in that order. The
-- repo-sync check is the one exception: it runs over the saved repo paths only,
-- matching the board indicator and `dispatch repo status`, so all three agree
-- on which repositories drift is measured for (see repo-sync.allium:
-- OneRepoSetForDriftMeasurement).
--
-- Flag availability is NOT uniform; each flag exists on specific command forms:
--   --repair    subcommand forms only (worktrees | sessions | hooks).
--               Detection stays the default. The bare `dispatch doctor`
--               all-checks form has no --repair, so it can never repair.
--               With --force: applies every repair_available finding and prints
--               one "repaired: {check} {target}" line per success (human mode).
--               Without --force: applies NOTHING. There is no confirmation
--               prompt. If at least one finding is repair_available it prints
--               the findings, then a "would repair" preview to stderr, then
--               exits 1. If no finding is repair_available it falls through to
--               the normal output path below.
--   --force     subcommand forms only. Not a prompt-skip: it is the only way to
--               actually apply repairs. Has no effect without --repair.
--   --dry-run   parent `doctor` command only; no per-subcommand form exists.
--               Forces detection-only by clearing --repair and --force.
--   --json      declared on both the parent command and each subcommand, with
--               the subcommand's value taking precedence. Because the
--               subcommand default is false, `dispatch doctor --json worktrees`
--               resolves to json = false — the parent flag is discarded.
--               Emits a JSON array of Finding records; one per check result.

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Worktrees check
------------------------------------------------------------

-- DB orphan: task.worktree is set but the path does not exist on disk
--   status: error, repair_available: true
--   repair: patch task to clear worktree and tmux_window columns

-- Disk orphan: a directory under {repo_path}/.worktrees/ exists on disk
--              but no task row has a matching worktree path
--   status: warn, repair_available: true
--   repair: git worktree remove --force <path>

-- Healthy state emits no finding at all. The worktrees and sessions checks are
-- silent-on-healthy: a task whose worktree path exists, a task with no worktree,
-- and a .worktrees/ dir whose every entry matches a DB row all produce nothing.
-- Neither check ever constructs an ok finding. Only the hooks check does, and it
-- emits one finding per known repo unconditionally.

-- Scan error: the {repo_path}/.worktrees/ directory exists but cannot be
--             enumerated (e.g. permission denied, or the path is not a
--             directory), or an individual entry fails to read mid-listing.
--             A simply-absent .worktrees/ directory (NotFound) is the healthy
--             common case and produces no finding.
--   status: warn, repair_available: false (nothing for doctor to auto-repair)

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Sessions check
------------------------------------------------------------

-- The check is task-driven, not tmux-driven: it iterates tasks, skips every task
-- whose tmux_window is unset, and for the rest compares window liveness against
-- task status. The live window list is only consulted as a membership test for a
-- window name the task already records.

-- Stale DB claim: task has tmux_window set and status in {running, review}
--                 but that window no longer exists in any tmux session
--   status: error, repair_available: true
--   repair: patch task to clear tmux_window column

-- Stale live window: task has tmux_window set, that window is still alive, and
--                    the task has status done or archived
--   status: warn, repair_available: true
--   repair: tmux kill-window -t <window>

-- Known gap: because the check never scans live tmux windows for unrecorded
-- names, a task row with tmux_window = null alongside a genuinely-live
-- "task-{id}" window is invisible to it — precisely the orphan a live-window
-- scan would catch.

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Hooks check
------------------------------------------------------------

-- Missing hooks config: git config core.hooksPath in repo != ".githooks"
--   status: warn, repair_available: true
--   repair: git config --local core.hooksPath .githooks (in the repo dir)

-- Hooks configured correctly:
--   status: ok, repair_available: false

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Repo-sync check
------------------------------------------------------------

-- Reports drift between each saved repo path's local base branch and its origin
-- counterpart. Deliberately the saved repo paths rather than doctor's wider
-- "known repo" union, so that doctor, the board indicator and
-- `dispatch repo status` all measure the same set (repo-sync.allium:
-- OneRepoSetForDriftMeasurement). The measurement, the base-branch resolution
-- and the sync operation itself all belong to repo-sync.allium; this check only
-- reports.

-- Drifted repo: the repo's ahead/behind measurement is known and at least one
--               side is non-zero (repo-sync.allium: RepoSyncState.has_drift)
--   target: the repo path
--   message: names the base branch and both counts
--   status: warn, repair_available: false
--
-- warn, never error: local base being ahead of origin is the NORMAL state
-- after every rebase wrap-up in this workflow, so an error here would make
-- `dispatch doctor` report a broken system for working exactly as designed.
--
-- repair_available: false is deliberate and not an oversight. Closing the gap
-- pushes to a shared branch, which repo-sync.allium's SyncNeverAutomatic
-- invariant reserves for an explicit operator action ([o] on the board, or
-- `dispatch repo sync`). Doctor diagnoses; it does not publish.

-- Unmeasurable repo: origin/<base> does not resolve, so no counts exist.
--   No finding. Silent-on-healthy in both directions: the check never claims a
--   repo is in sync, and never reports drift it could not measure. See the
--   UnmeasuredIsNeverPresentedAsClean invariant in repo-sync.allium.

-- In-sync repo (both counts zero): no finding. Like worktrees and sessions,
-- and unlike hooks, this check constructs no ok finding.

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Output contract
------------------------------------------------------------

-- Default (human) output: one line per Finding
--   "{status}  {check}  {target}  {message}"
--   where status is padded to 5 chars: "ok   " | "warn " | "error"
--   When the finding list is empty, the literal line "all checks passed" is
--   printed instead. This substitution is human-mode only; --json emits an
--   empty array rather than that line.
--
-- --json output: a flat JSON array; one object per Finding
--   [{"check":"...","status":"...","target":"...","message":"...","repair_available":...}]
--
-- Exit code has three distinct modes:
--   plain check run (no --repair, or --repair with nothing repairable):
--     0 when no finding is warn or error; 1 when any finding is warn or error.
--   --repair --force: 0 as long as every attempted repair succeeded, even when
--     the original findings included errors — the errors are what was repaired.
--     1 only when a repair itself fails.
--   --repair without --force (preview path): 1 whenever at least one finding is
--     repair_available, regardless of severity, since nothing was resolved.

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Open Questions
------------------------------------------------------------

open question "The repo-sync check reports drift as warn, but the plain-check exit rule maps any warn to exit 1 — and local base being ahead of origin is the normal state after every rebase wrap-up. As specified, `dispatch doctor` therefore exits non-zero almost permanently, which the check was explicitly meant not to cause. Which gives way: does repo-sync drift get a severity that does not affect the exit code, is it excluded from the exit-code calculation, or does the workflow accept a habitually non-zero doctor?"

open question "Does the repo-sync check fetch before measuring? Without a fetch its counts are only as fresh as the last dispatch or wrap-up in that repo, so a repo behind origin can be reported as in sync — and no fetch error exists for it to report either. With a fetch, `dispatch doctor` gains network I/O and a per-repo latency cost that no other check has. `dispatch repo status` settles the same question with a --no-fetch flag; doctor has no equivalent."

open question "repo-sync is the only check with no subcommand form: it runs only in the bare all-checks run, so an operator cannot ask about drift alone the way they can about worktrees, sessions or hooks. Should it gain a `dispatch doctor repo-sync` form for symmetry, given it has nothing to repair and `dispatch repo status` already offers a richer per-repo view?"
